  "assumptions": [
    "All values are synthetic and illustrative, not a real HAPS design or measured weather profile.",
    "The case comprises two consecutive 24-hour days represented as 48 contiguous one-hour intervals.",
    "Day 1 has 800 W/m2 irradiance during twelve daylight hours; Day 2 has 1,000 W/m2 during twelve daylight hours. Both nights have zero irradiance.",
    "PV power is incident irradiance times 1.25 m2 array area, 20% PV efficiency and unity incidence, temperature, MPPT and wiring factors.",
    "Day 1 own load is 100 W for all 24 hours. Day 2 own load is 100 W during daylight and 50 W at night.",
    "The HAPS battery begins at 1,000 Wh, capacity is 1,800 Wh and the hard reserve is 600 Wh. Battery charge and discharge efficiencies are unity for this arithmetic fixture.",
    "The HAPS serves own loads, charges its battery, then exports only remaining PV surplus at 80% end-to-end link efficiency; the battery never funds external export.",
    "Day 1 ends at 600 Wh and does not independently close its daily cycle. Day 2 recovers the state to 1,200 Wh, so the complete two-day horizon closes above its 1,000 Wh initial state.",
    "Per-day closure and horizon closure are deliberately distinct: daily_energy_cycle_closed is false because Day 1 fails to recover within that day; horizon_energy_cycle_closed is true.",
    "This case tests arithmetic scheduling only; it does not model correlated real weather, battery ageing, thermal dynamics, wind, buoyancy, aircraft propulsion, coverage or flight safety."
  ],
  "claim_ceiling": "synthetic_arithmetic_oracle"
